DERIVED WORK — TORAX (Apache-2.0)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The scrape-off-layer module is a PORT of TORAX's edge implementation, not an
independent implementation from the papers it cites.  What was taken is
upstream's: the fitted coefficient tables, the initial guesses, the fixed
iteration count and the ORDER of the fixed-point updates — each stated at its
use site in the source, because each of them changes the answer.

    Upstream:  google-deepmind/torax, revision b4d40633 (TORAX_VERSION 1.4.3)
    Terms:     Apache License 2.0
    Authors:   the TORAX contributors

Files in this distribution that derive from it:

    rust/fylite/src/edge_tables.rs <- the Mavrin-2017 non-coronal fit
                                      coefficients for eight low-Z impurities
                                      (mean charge state and cooling rate),
                                      transcribed from upstream's own table
                                      (collisional_radiative_models) through a
                                      frozen record
    rust/fylite/src/edge.rs        <- the evaluation of those fits, and the
                                      extended Lengyel model: divertor_sol_1d
                                      (the two-point state and its derived
                                      properties), extended_lengyel_formulas
                                      (the closed forms and calc_Z_eff),
                                      extended_lengyel_solvers (the forward and
                                      inverse fixed points) and
                                      extended_lengyel_defaults (initial
                                      guesses, the 25-pass iteration count, the
                                      L_INT quadrature resolution)

STATEMENT OF CHANGES (Apache-2.0 section 4b).  The port is a translation into
Rust, not a copy: arrays become plain `&[f64]` with no array framework under
them, the coefficient tables are generated into the source rather than read at
runtime, and a model that cannot answer returns an explicit `PhysicsOutcome`
rather than a NaN.  The PHYSICS of both halves is the cited literature — Mavrin,
J Fusion Energ 36 (2017) 161; Body et al., Nucl. Fusion 65 (2025) 086002; Eich
et al., Nucl. Fusion 60 (2020) 056016; Stangeby, PPCF 60 (2018) 044022 — quoted
at each formula.  What is TORAX's is the implementation's own choices, listed
above.

★TORAX is ALSO an ORACLE for parts of this distribution that do NOT derive from
it, and the two relationships look alike from a test file.  `zerod.rs`'s
Bosch-Hale reactivity and `neoclassical.rs`'s Redl-2021 coefficients are
independent implementations of the same PAPERS, measured against TORAX's
(docs/cases/registry.jsonld, record V-08); the QLKNN flux-combination layer
compared in V-08 / V-14 / B-05 lives in the public repository, not here.  An
oracle is a thing you are measured against, not a thing you copied — the same
distinction the fytrans notice above draws for `transport.rs`.

