DERIVED WORK — VEQPy (BSD-3-Clause)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The parametric fixed-boundary Grad-Shafranov solve is a PORT of VEQPy's
discrete system, not an independent implementation from the paper alone.

    Upstream:  FusionAlpha/veqpy, https://github.com/FusionAlpha/veqpy,
               revision 2b64c9a
    Method:    R. Zhang, H. Xie, Y. Li, W. Meng, F. Wang, Z. Wang, "VEQ: a fast
               parametric Grad-Shafranov solver for fixed-boundary tokamak
               equilibria with flexible source profiles", arXiv:2606.11821v1
    Terms:     BSD 3-Clause (reproduced below)
    Authors:   rhzhang and the VEQPy contributors

Files in this distribution that derive from it:

    rust/fylite/src/veq.rs         <- the MXH-Chebyshev map and its metric
                                      (geometry_stage.py), the profile families
                                      and their axis powers (profile_stage.py,
                                      packed_layout.py), the transformed residual
                                      and its variational projection
                                      (numba_residual.py), the PF / psi_N source
                                      route with the I_p constraint and its axis
                                      regularisations (numba_source.py,
                                      numba_operator.py), the radial calculus on
                                      Gauss-Legendre nodes (numerics/), the
                                      least-squares boundary model
                                      (kernels/boundary_fit.py) and the cold
                                      start (initialize.py)

STATEMENT OF CHANGES.  Python/Numba to Rust, PF route only (the PP, PI, PJ1,
PJ2 and PQ routes and the beta constraint are not ported).  The flux is carried
in this project's full-turn gauge instead of VEQPy's per-radian one, the source
tables are read linearly (fixedbnd's convention) instead of by local barycentric
interpolation — identical on the linear tables the agreement tests use — and
the prefix-integration matrix comes from the discrete orthogonality of the
Legendre polynomials rather than a Vandermonde solve.  ★The NONLINEAR SOLVER IS
NOT VEQPy's: VEQPy calls SciPy's MINPACK-derived `hybr` (and ships a C++ port of
it); nothing of either was read for, or copied into, this distribution.  The
Levenberg-Marquardt solve in veq.rs (Broyden updates carried on a QR by plane
rotations) is written from the textbooks it cites.

